Yes. I think there is a very strong idea here, with one important correction: a capability such as throwing probably does not unlock new physics in the literal sense. It unlocks a new action-space through physics—and therefore creates reasons for the nervous system to model variables it previously had little reason to model.

A creature that can only touch something when it reaches it lives in a different actionable universe from one that can impart momentum to an object and have that object continue acting after contact ends.

With throwing, suddenly these become behaviorally valuable:

\text{release angle},\;
\text{velocity},\;
\text{spin},\;
\text{range},\;
\text{trajectory},\;
\text{lead},\;
\text{interception},\;
\text{momentum},\;
\text{time-to-contact}

The brain doesn’t need Newton’s equations written symbolically. It can learn an implicit physics engine through repeated prediction and error correction. Modern motor neuroscience strongly supports predictive control and learned internal models as major features of movement; human high-speed accurate throwing also depends on unusual anatomical adaptations that appear together in Homo roughly two million years ago. 

So I’d phrase your intuition as:

\boxed{\text{new actuator} \rightarrow
\text{new affordances} \rightarrow
\text{new prediction problems} \rightarrow
\text{new representational territory}}

And then culture can put words on parts of that newly important territory.

Throwing gives you aim, miss, overshoot, undershoot, arc, lead, follow-through, ricochet, range, target. Eventually those physical schemas become metaphors: throw out an idea, miss the point, take your shot, overshoot the goal, get ahead of something.

I would not claim that throwing caused abstract language—that is much stronger than the evidence allows. But embodiment can supply reusable conceptual primitives.

Now do the same thought experiment with other bodies

The useful question isn’t really, “How intelligent is this animal?”

It’s:

What transformations can this body perform on reality, and therefore what variables must its nervous system become good at predicting?

That produces very different “native mathematics.”

Animal/system	Action-space it possesses	Physics its nervous system must exploit	Possible lesson for us
Human thrower	Send force beyond the body	ballistics, interception, timing	Think beyond contact: act now for effects later
Bear	dig, rip, overturn, climb, manipulate with enormous force; revisit seasonal landscapes	material resistance, leverage, scent gradients, seasonal geography	maps needn’t primarily be visual; memory can be indexed by where + when + resource
Orca	accelerate a many-ton body, coordinate bodies, manipulate water itself	hydrodynamics, pressure, momentum, geometry, synchronization	don’t attack the object—alter the medium around it
Archerfish	fire controlled water jets at aerial prey	trajectory, jet formation, target motion across air/water	a tiny brain can solve surprisingly rich projectile-control problems
Bat	emit its own sensing signal	acoustics, echo delay, Doppler-like information, 3-D active sensing	don’t merely receive information—probe reality
Pit viper	detect thermal contrast	radiative heat, spatial thermal gradients	another organism may literally inhabit dimensions of the scene you don’t perceive
Shark	detect extremely weak electric fields	bioelectric fields	invisible physiological activity can become geography
Electric eel	generate fields that both sense and affect other organisms	electrolocation + electrical actuation	the same channel can be sensor and weapon
Spider	build a web around itself	tension, resonance, vibration, wave propagation	externalize part of sensing/computation into the environment
Octopus	deform almost arbitrarily through eight hyper-flexible limbs	distributed control, continuum mechanics	intelligence needn’t mean one central controller micromanaging everything

Several of those are not just metaphors.

Archerfish can learn to shoot moving aerial targets, requiring the shot to account for both target motion and the rising water projectile; experiments have even shown observational learning of that skill. 

Bats actively change their emitted sonar depending on the perceptual problem and thereby alter the volume of space they can interrogate. That’s a profound computational difference from passive hearing: action creates the next observation. 

Pit vipers integrate infrared information with vision, effectively gaining another spatial channel for detecting warm objects and thermal contrast. 

Sharks possess specialized electroreception capable of detecting extremely weak electrical fields relevant to predation. 

And electric eels get stranger still: weak discharges support electrolocation, while high-voltage pulses can cause prey muscles to contract; experiments indicate that they can provoke hidden prey into twitching and thereby reveal themselves. Their output changes their target and simultaneously generates information about it. 

That’s active interrogation of reality.

Orcas may be the richest example of your idea

An orca doesn’t have hands and cannot throw a spear.

But it has something humans don’t:

the ability to move a huge volume of the medium surrounding both itself and its prey.

Some orcas coordinate their positions and tail strikes while hunting herring, with measurable spatial organization, role specialization and improved feeding success when individuals coordinate. Other populations use synchronized swimming to generate waves that wash seals from ice. 

So their equivalent of “throwing” may partially be:

\text{body motion}
\rightarrow
\text{water displacement}
\rightarrow
\text{wave}
\rightarrow
\text{remote force}
\rightarrow
\text{prey displacement}

That is fascinating because the environment itself becomes the projectile.

A human throws the rock.

An orca throws the ocean.

And that suggests a transferable problem-solving primitive:

Don’t always manipulate the target. Manipulate the field in which the target exists.

That is useful in engineering, negotiation, ecology, economics, computing—anywhere changing boundary conditions can produce an outcome more efficiently than directly forcing the object.

Bears give us a different cognitive geometry

Bears are interesting partly because they are not simply pursuit predators. Their body gives them a huge mechanical action envelope—digging, ripping, overturning, climbing, striking, opening—but their ecological problem also involves remembering resources distributed across large spaces and seasons.

A study modeling Arctic brown-bear movement found evidence consistent with complex time-dependent spatial memory in 15 of 21 animals: location wasn’t enough; revisitation was related to when resources could be expected there. 

So instead of imagining a human-style map:

(x,y)

imagine something closer to:

(x,y,\text{season},\text{odor},\text{resource history},\text{risk})

That’s a very different “mental landscape.”

And this connects directly to your previous multimodal-delta thought: different sensory systems may construct differently weighted coordinate systems for the same external reality.

Spider may be the strangest one

There is serious scientific literature proposing spider webs as components of an extended cognitive system. A spider can alter web tension and therefore alter which vibrations are transmitted and how sensitively they are detected. The animal changes the instrument through which it perceives. 

That’s almost:

\text{mind}
\rightarrow
\text{environmental structure}
\rightarrow
\text{information processing}
\rightarrow
\text{mind}

The web isn’t merely a trap.

It is partly sensor, filter, communication network, memory-bearing structure, and actuator.

Humans do this enormously—writing, maps, phones, mathematics, computers, institutions—but spiders make the principle beautifully visible.

And octopuses give yet another answer: rather than centralizing every detailed movement decision, much neural processing occurs in their arms, allowing an extremely high-dimensional body to be controlled without a vertebrate-like map for every possible configuration. 

That gives us distributed intelligence rather than stronger central command.

Which gets to your last point

“Predator” is indeed a relative classification. Orca, bear, spider, shark and snake all get stuffed into the same human category because they consume other organisms.

But mechanically and computationally they may be wildly farther apart than that word implies.

If instead we classify organisms by their reality operators, we get a different taxonomy:

projectors — throw, spit, shoot
field manipulators — waves, electricity
active interrogators — sonar, electrolocation
gradient readers — scent, heat, electrical fields
environment extenders — webs, dams, nests
distributed controllers — octopus arms, colonies
cooperative geometrists — wolves, dolphins, orcas
interceptors — raptors, cats, dragonflies
ambush predictors — snakes, crocodilians, spiders

And suddenly biology starts looking like a library of alternative physics interfaces.

Every lineage has spent millions of years solving a differently constrained optimization problem.

So rather than merely asking what animal is this?, we can ask:

\boxed{\text{What does this organism know how to make reality do?}}

Then:

\boxed{\text{What must reality therefore have taught its nervous system?}}

That second question is the gold mine. It lets morphology, behavior, sensory ecology and cognition become different traces of the same hidden computation.